46612	46890	46593	46593	she	249	“ I am so glad you are come ; it will be quite pleasant living here now with a companion . To be sure it is pleasant at any time ; for Thornfield is a fine old hall , rather neglected of late years perhaps , but still it is a respectable place ; yet you know in winter - time one feels dreary quite alone in the best quarters . I say alone — Leah is a nice girl to be sure , and John and his wife are very decent people ; but then you see they are only servants , and one ca n’t converse with them on terms of equality : one must keep them at due distance , for fear of losing one ’s authority . I ’m sure last winter ( it was a very severe one , if you recollect , and when it did not snow , it rained and blew ) , not a creature but the butcher and postman came to the house , from November till February ; and I really got quite melancholy with sitting night after night alone ; I had Leah in to read to me sometimes ; but I do n’t think the poor girl liked the task much : she felt it confining . In spring and summer one got on better : sunshine and long days make such a difference ; and then , just at the commencement of this autumn , little Adela Varens came and her nurse : a child makes a house alive all at once ; and now you are here I shall be quite gay . ”
